@packages/compiler/src/evaluation-execution.sigil import { SigilAgentExecutionPolicy }
@packages/compiler/src/adapter-execution-coordinator.sigil import { SigilAgentAdapterExecutionCoordinator }

component SigilAgentAdapterSubprocess {
  goal {
    Own provider subprocess launch, output-channel settlement, and bounded cleanup
    shared by CLI adapter implementations.
  }

  interface {
    MechanicalCommandResult {
      A host mechanical check runner may request the actual nonzero exit result.
      Default provider invocations still reject nonzero exits. Launch, timeout,
      cancellation, output and cleanup failures remain operational failures in
      both modes; a failed command is never converted into a passing result.
    }
    AdapterSubprocessFailure {
      contains a machine-readable kind that is cancelled, elapsed-time,
      preventive-budget, operational-limit, process, or process-cleanup

      process-cleanup contains process identity, graceful and forced attempts,
      observed exit and channel states, and an operator recovery action; process may
      contain exit status and the complete verbatim stderr from a nonzero provider
      exit
    }

    AdapterSubprocess {
      validateInput(input, AgentOperationalLimits) returns success or
      operational-limit and owns initial request size calculation

      run(command, args, input, AgentExecutionBudgets, AgentOperationalLimits,
      AgentCancellation, AdapterExecutionHandle, AdapterExecutionResources,
      terminationControl, onFrame) declares one unstarted handle before launch,
      attaches at most one process, delivers identified stdout and stderr frames in
      per-channel order, and reports process status, channel settlement, and
      provider-specific failures to the coordinator

      AdapterExecutionHandle, AdapterExecutionResources, and terminationControl are
      coordinator-owned required inputs; run never creates fallback lifecycle
      ownership or executes without them

      terminationControl may request preventive-budget termination during a run

      input exceeding maxInitialRequestChars rejects as operational-limit before
      launch; a frame exceeding maxProviderFrameChars becomes operational-limit after
      launch; onFrame backpressure bounds unread host buffering

      successful settlement occurs only after process exit and designated stdout and
      stderr end-of-stream

      Output decoding or frame-consumer failure preserves its initiating failure
      while independently cancelling and observing the affected stream. A rejected
      result is not itself evidence that process cleanup failed.
    }
  }
}

expand SigilAgentAdapterSubprocess {
  logic {
    AdapterSubprocess {
      Do not launch when cancellation is already requested or initial input exceeds
      its limit. Declare the unstarted AdapterExecutionHandle before launch and
      attach process and channel declarations immediately after launch. After launch,
      deliver bounded channel-identified frames with per-channel ordering and
      backpressure.

      After a nonzero provider exit and both output channels settle, retain complete
      stderr in the process failure even when an onFrame consumer already observed
      that channel. Do not apply a retained-character limit to that failure evidence.

      For a non-result process transition, close input, request graceful termination,
      and wait only within the remaining coordinator cleanup deadline for process
      exit and channel closure.

      If graceful cleanup has not settled within the remaining deadline, request
      forced termination, cancel designated output channels, and continue only until
      that same deadline expires.

      If forced cleanup also expires, unreference the process and report deadline
      expiry with recovery evidence; the coordinator selects the caller outcome.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    CleanupSettlement {
      Process cleanup receives the coordinator-selected absolute monotonic deadline
      through AdapterExecutionHandle, reports process and channel settlement to the
      owning execution coordinator, and does not select a terminal outcome.

      Platform signal behavior may differ, but both cleanup phases and their observed
      process and channel states remain explicit.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    CoordinatorBoundSubprocessInvocation {
      Decision: Require every subprocess run to receive the coordinator-created
      execution handle, resource hooks, and termination control.

      Scope: Governs subprocess invocation and cleanup-observation ownership;
      excludes provider command construction and platform termination mechanics.

      Trade-offs: Direct subprocess callers must create a coordinator-compatible
      execution context.

      Design issues addressed: Optional lifecycle hooks permit an unobserved process
      to bypass bounded cleanup verification.

      Consequences: Every provider process and result input is declared, observed,
      and recoverable through one coordinator-owned lifecycle.
    }

    TwoPhaseProviderCleanup {
      Decision: Attempt graceful and then forced provider termination within one
      total providerCleanupMs deadline and make unverified cleanup a distinct
      failure.

      Scope: Governs owned provider processes and designated output channels.

      Trade-offs: Forced termination receives only the graceful phase's remaining
      time, and an unverified process may be unreferenced only after recovery
      evidence is retained.

      Design issues addressed: A termination signal does not prove process exit or
      output closure.

      Discarded alternatives: Infinite waiting, immediate detachment, and preserving
      the earlier outcome after unverified cleanup were rejected.

      Consequences: Failure settlement is bounded and never overstates cleanup.
    }

    FailedProviderStderrEvidence {
      Decision: Preserve complete verbatim stderr from a nonzero provider exit in the
      process failure instead of discarding it after onFrame delivery.

      Scope: Governs failed provider subprocess diagnostics; excludes successful
      result framing, per-frame limits, and cleanup recovery evidence.

      Trade-offs: Compiler reports can become large and expose provider-produced
      diagnostic text.

      Design issues addressed: A provider exit code without stderr prevents hosts
      from diagnosing evaluator failure.

      Discarded alternatives: Truncation, omission after frame delivery, and a
      separate host-only log were rejected because they can hide the causal failure.

      Consequences: A failed compiler report contains the provider's complete error
      text for user diagnosis.
    }
  }

  cases {
    AdapterSubprocess {
      Graceful exit within providerCleanupMs preserves the selected terminal failure.

      An oversized initial input starts no process; an oversized provider frame
      triggers bounded cleanup after launch.

      A process surviving graceful cleanup receives forced termination and channel
      cancellation.

      Failure to verify exit after forced cleanup returns process-cleanup failure and
      retained recovery evidence.

      A nonzero provider exit after stderr output reports that complete stderr in its
      process failure even when a frame consumer received the same text.
    }
  }
}
